! mcp_bootstrap_p3.strap -- MCP's IPL BOOTSTRAP, printed page 3.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.10, header "PAGE NUMBER 3".
! Continues programs/mcp_bootstrap_p2.strap exactly: p.2's last
! instruction is at 000052.00 and is a half word, and p.3's first is at
! 000052.40.  docs/boot_plan.md M3.
!
! WHAT THIS PAGE IS.  Three banners divide it.
!
!   FIND IPL CHANNEL NUMBER (lines 1-11).  The bootstrap does not know
!   which channel it was loaded from, so it walks the channels: M+1
!   adds one to the 19-bit field at the head of X1 CCW -- the channel
!   address field, whose seven-bit channel number sits at IBM bits
!   12-18 of a 24-bit address (Reference Manual p.111), so bit 18 is
!   the channel's units digit and adding one at the bottom of a 19-bit
!   field is exactly "ADVANCE CHANNEL".  Each pass copies that
!   channel's control word to X IPL BS and tests bit 63 of it, the
!   refill field's low bit: p.2's own comment on the IPL control word
!   says "ONE IN REFILL FIELD IDENTIFIES IPLCW".  The walk starts at
!   15.32 = 992 = 31 << 5, i.e. channel 31, one below the first
!   channel of the basic exchange (printed p.5 line 21 enters that one
!   as "(8)20.0", 1024 = 32 << 5).
!
!   IO TEST (lines 13-30).  The status loop every I-O operation in
!   this program returns to: SIC plants a return address, three
!   branch-on-indicator tests take the three rejection cases (exchange
!   check, unit not ready, channel busy), then CCW fetches the
!   channel's control word and BB tests bit 24 -- SEOP, "the operation
!   completed".  CT0011 then tests the status bits at X IPL BS+.19
!   without storing a result (Connect for Test), and BRZ branches on
!   the result being zero: no status bits on, success.
!
!   SPACE OVER TAPE MARK (lines 32-52).  SPFL(SEOP) spaces the tape
!   forward past a tape mark, the same status loop runs, and
!   REL(SEOP) clears the EE and EOP status bits the spacing set.  THIS
!   IS THE LINE THAT FIXES THE TAPE LAYOUT: the bootstrap record is
!   followed by a tape mark, and the next thing follows that.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! SUBSTITUTIONS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) BLANKS INSIDE SYMBOL NAMES ARE DROPPED, as on p.2.  C22-6531
!     printed p.10 allows "six or fewer alphabetic and numeric
!     characters" and requires "only alphameric characters", and every
!     name on this page is six or fewer once the blanks come out:
!     X1, X1 CCW, X ERR 00, X7 1, X ERR 02..05, X7 1 CCW, X7 3, X8,
!     X8 1, X8 2, X ER 05A..05E.  Note that the listing drops a letter
!     from "X ERR" to "X ER" exactly when the number grows a third
!     character -- X ERR 05 but X ER 05A -- which is the six-character
!     limit being obeyed in IBM's own hand.
!
! (2) INDEX REGISTER 15 IS WRITTEN "$X15" WHERE THE LISTING WRITES A
!     BARE "15" (line 10, "LVE,15,X1 CCW") or "$15" (lines 23, 35, 42,
!     50).  Both reach the same four bits; p.2 made the same
!     substitution for "CB+,15,X CLSTO".
!
! (3) LETTER O VERSUS DIGIT 0 IN "XER05E".  Line 46's operand is
!     printed without blanks, as one word, and its third-from-last
!     glyph is the one the printer renders alike for letter O and
!     digit zero.  It is a reference to line 48's label, printed
!     "X ER 05E", and that label sits in the run X ERR 00, 02, 03, 04,
!     05, then X ER 05A, 05B, 05C, 05D, 05E -- a numbering, so the
!     glyph is a digit.  Whichever it is, the two must agree, and they
!     do here.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ONE ROW THAT DOES NOT CHECK -- line 6, and read the note
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The listing prints, for "X1 CCW  CCW,15.32,X IPL BS":
!
!     000053.40   000017.40 30   000041.21 00
!
! Everything in that except two bits is confirmed.  The 24-bit channel
! address is 000017.40 = 992 = 31 << 5, which is the channel the walk
! starts from.  The second half-word is a textbook COPY CONTROL WORD:
! 000041 is X IPL BS as a word address, and .21 = 010001 is bits 50-55
! -- the unused bit 50, then OP = 1000 = CCW, then the first bit of
! the literal 10000.  But bits 24-27 print as 0011 where X22-6533's
! INPUT-OUTPUT OPERATIONS format requires the literal 1000, and this
! assembler emits 80 where the page prints 30.
!
! THE GLYPH IS NOT THE EXPLANATION, and that was checked rather than
! assumed, because this very page caught the opposite error one row
! further on: line 10's trailing byte reads 80 at 900 dpi and is B0,
! which the opcode arithmetic settles outright (LVE is 11011, so bits
! 24-27 must be 1011).  Line 6's byte was rendered at 1800 dpi beside
! line 7's 80 in a single crop: the 3 is open on the left and the 8
! closed, in the same render, on adjacent rows.  It is a 3.
!
! WHAT IS DISTINCTIVE about line 6 is that it is the only I-O
! instruction in this corpus whose channel operand is written as a
! plain literal rather than as an indexed "0($15)" or "0.0($1)" -- and
! every indexed one prints 8 in that nibble (lines 23, 35, 42 and 50
! here; printed p.4 line 5; printed p.5 lines 28, 36, 39, 45, 48, 52).
! So this row is left OUT of the checked set, named in
! verify_mcp_bootstrap_p3.py, rather than quietly bent to fit.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS.  EVERY SYN IS AN INPUT TO THE CHECK, NOT A WITNESS -- a
! SYN makes its own address come out right by construction.  All three
! are taken from the page that DEFINES them, not from this page's own
! printed machine code, which would make the rows that reference them
! prove nothing:
!   X IPL BS  printed p.2 line 24, "X IPL BS  BD,X BEGIN", 000041.00
!   X ERROR   printed p.5 line 5, "X ERROR  LX,$2,X ERR IN", 000133.00
!   X ERR AD  printed p.13 line 37, "X ERR AD  VF,0", 000551.00
XIPLBS  SYN, (8)41.0
XERROR  SYN, (8)133.0
XERRAD  SYN, (8)551.0

        SLC, (8)52.40      ! line 5's location; p.2 ends at 000052.00
                            ! with a half word

! ---- FIND IPL CHANNEL NUMBER --------------------------------------
X1      M+1(BU,19), X1CCW       ! line 5   - ADVANCE CHANNEL
X1CCW   CCW, 15.32, XIPLBS      ! line 6   -- see the note above; this
                                 ! row is NOT in the checked set
XERR00  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 7
        BEKJZ, XERROR           ! line 8   -IF EXCHANGE ERROR
        BZB, XIPLBS+.63, X1     ! line 9   -IS IT IPL CHANNEL
        LVE, $X15, X1CCW        ! line 10  - OBTAIN NUMBER OF IPL CHANNEL
        B, X8                   ! line 11  -TO SPACE OVER TAPE MARK

! ---- IO TEST ------------------------------------------------------
X71     SIC, XERRAD             ! line 17
        BEKJZ, XERROR           ! line 18  -IF EXCHANGE ERROR
XERR02  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 19
        BUNRJZ, XERROR          ! line 20  -UNIT NOT READY REJECT
XERR03  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 21
        BCBJZ, XERROR           ! line 22  -IF CHANNEL BUSY REJECT
X71CCW  CCW, 0($X15), XIPLBS    ! line 23
XERR04  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 24
        BEKJZ, XERROR           ! line 25  -IF EXCHANGE ERROR FOR CCW
        BB, XIPLBS+.24, X71CCW  ! line 26  -IO OPERATION COMPLETED
        CT0011(BU,5), XIPLBS+.19 ! line 27 -- CT, Connect for Test:
                                 ! the result goes nowhere and only the
                                 ! indicators are set (X22-6533
                                 ! CONNECTIVES, xxxx10111), which is
                                 ! why the next row can branch on it.
X73     BRZ, 0.0                ! line 28  -SUCCESSFUL
XERR05  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 29
        B, XERROR               ! line 30  -IO STATUS BIT ON

! ---- SPACE OVER TAPE MARK -----------------------------------------
X8      SPFL(SEOP), 0.0($X15)   ! line 35  -SPACE OVER TAPE MARK
XER05A  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 36
        BEKJZ, XERROR           ! line 37  -IF EXCHANGE ERROR
XER05B  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 38
        BUNRJZ, XERROR          ! line 39  -IF UNIT NOT READY REJECT
XER05C  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 40
        BCBJZ, XERROR           ! line 41  -IF CHANNEL BUSY REJECT
X81     CCW, 0($X15), XIPLBS    ! line 42  -GET STATUS BITS
XER05D  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 43
        BEKJZ, XERROR           ! line 44  -IF EXCHANGE ERROR
        BB, XIPLBS+.24, X81     ! line 45  -IF SEOP OFF
        BZBZ, XIPLBS+.21, XER05E ! line 46 -OFF * IF EE ON
        BZB, XIPLBS+.19, X82    ! line 47  -YES*TEST FOR UK
XER05E  SIC, XERRAD             ! line 48
        B, XERROR               ! line 49  -ERROR IN SPACING
X82     REL(SEOP), 0.0($X15)    ! line 50  -CLEAR EE AND EOP STATUS BIT
        SIC, X73                ! line 51
        B, X71                  ! line 52  - TO IO TEST
        END, X1

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! AN UNEXPLAINED MARKER IN THE LOCATION COLUMN, recorded because it
! will recur on the eleven pages still to transcribe.
!
! Three rows on this page print an asterisk AFTER the location:
!   line  5  000052.40 *   X1      M+1(BU,19),X1 CCW
!   line 28  000066.40 *   X7 3    BRZ,0.0
!   line 50  000102.00 *   X8 2    REL(SEOP),0.0($15)
! and it is not confined to this page -- printed p.2 line 5
! (000051.34 *), p.4 lines 20 and 38, p.5 lines 26 and 46, p.13 lines
! 6 and 20 all carry it, roughly two or three per page.
!
! WHAT IT IS NOT.  It is not the asterisk every LINE number carries.
! It is not a gap marker: at 200 dpi line 27 appeared to sit at
! 000065.00, which would have left a half word unused before line 28,
! but the location column at 600 dpi reads 000065.40 and line 27's
! full word ends exactly where line 28 begins.  None of the eight
! instances follows a gap.  It does not track the card-identification
! column either -- line 5's card is 11000045, between line 4's 44 and
! line 6's 46 -- nor "second statement punched on one card", which
! lines 7 and 8 are without carrying it.  The eight instances have no
! operation, operand form, index register or alignment in common.
!
! WHAT IT CANNOT BE.  It cannot change the assembled bits, because it
! sits in the LOCATION column and all three of this page's instances
! are in the checked set below and match the listing's own BINARY
! OUTPUT.  So it is listing metadata of some kind; which kind is open.
